---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
---
<ToolLayout
  tool="spacing"
  intro="Enter the bed size and the spacing from your seed packet or plant label. You get the number of rows, plants per row, a top-down plan drawn in proportion, and the measurements for marking it out."
  related={[
    { id: 'soil', why: 'work out how much soil the same bed needs.' },
    { id: 'seed', why: 'turn the packet’s sowing times into dates for the plants you have just laid out.' },
    { id: 'mulch', why: 'estimate mulch for the bed once it is planted.' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>How the layout is worked out</h2>
  <p>Everything here follows one simple, stated model, so you can check it with a tape measure.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Spacing is centre to centre.</strong> The distance is from the middle of one plant to the middle of the next, which is how seed packets and plant labels give it.</li>
    <li><strong>Edge clearance comes off both ends.</strong> The usable run along each side is the bed dimension minus the clearance twice.</li>
    <li><strong>The first plant sits at the start of the usable run.</strong> With a clearance of 6 in, the first centre is exactly 6 in from the edge. With no clearance, the first centre is on the edge line itself.</li>
    <li><strong>One more position fits for every full spacing step.</strong> If the usable run is 84 in and plants are 12 in apart, that is 7 steps and so 8 plants.</li>
    <li><strong>Rows work the same way</strong> across the other side of the bed, using the row spacing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Positions are counted along each side separately. The calculator never divides the bed area by an “area per plant”, because that overcounts in beds whose sides are not a neat multiple of the spacing.
  </p>

  <h3>Choosing an edge clearance</h3>
  <p>
    A plant whose centre is on the edge of a bed has half its space outside it. Many gardeners therefore keep the outer plants about half a spacing in from the edge. That is a layout convention, not a rule: enter whatever suits your bed, or leave it blank for none.
  </p>

  <h3>Leftover space</h3>
  <p>
    When the usable run is not an exact multiple of the spacing, some space is left. By default it stays at the far end. Tick “Centre the layout” to share it equally between both ends. The number of plants is the same either way.
  </p>

  <h2>Staggered rows</h2>
  <p>
    Tick “Stagger alternate rows” to shift every second row along by half a spacing, so each plant sits opposite the gap in the row before. The shifted rows still have to respect the edge clearance at the far end, so they often hold one plant fewer; the results give both counts and both sets of marks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Staggering on its own does not change the row spacing you entered. If you want every neighbour the same distance away (an even triangle pattern), use the button that sets the row spacing to 0.866 × the plant spacing. That fits more rows into the bed, but only do it if your packet’s row spacing allows rows that close.
  </p>

  <h2>Row direction</h2>
  <p>
    Rows can run along the length of the bed or across its width. Because in-row spacing and row spacing usually differ, the two directions often hold different numbers of plants. The results always show the count for the other direction, with a button to switch.
  </p>

  <h2>How many seeds to buy</h2>
  <p>
    Open “Seeds needed” and enter what your packet tells you. There are two common ways to sow:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One seed per position.</strong> Leave “Seeds sown at each position” blank (or enter 1) and enter the germination rate. The calculator divides the positions by the germination rate, so 24 positions at 80% need 24 ÷ 0.8 = 30 seeds for one plant per position on average.</li>
    <li><strong>Several seeds per position, thinned later.</strong> Enter 2 or 3. Seeds needed is simply positions × seeds per position. With a germination rate, the results also show the chance a position gets no seedling at all: at 70% germination and 3 seeds, that is 0.3 × 0.3 × 0.3 = 2.7%.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Add your own extra for losses if you want a margin, and the seeds per packet to see how many packets to buy. Packet germination rates come from tests in good conditions, so outdoors the real rate is often lower.
  </p>
  <p>
    The results end with a link that starts a sowing schedule for the same crop in the seed starting calculator.
  </p>

  <h2>Marking out the bed</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Use the “Measurements for marking out” table. Mark the row centres along one side of the bed, measuring from one edge.</li>
    <li>Run a string line for each row.</li>
    <li>Along each string, mark the plant centres, measuring from the same end each time.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The plan is drawn in proportion to your measurements, but a printout is not true to scale. Always work from the written measurements.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="more">
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <div class="worked">
      <p><strong>An 8 ft × 4 ft bed. The packet says 12 in between plants and 18 in between rows. Edge clearance 6 in.</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rows along the length: the usable length is 96 − 2 × 6 = 84 in. 84 ÷ 12 = 7 steps, so <strong>8 plants per row</strong>, at 6, 18, 30 … 90 in from one end.</li>
        <li>The usable width is 48 − 2 × 6 = 36 in. 36 ÷ 18 = 2 steps, so <strong>3 rows</strong>, at 6, 24 and 42 in from one long side.</li>
        <li>Total: 3 × 8 = <strong>24 positions</strong>.</li>
        <li>Staggered, rows along the length: the second row starts at 6 + 6 = 12 in and must stop by 90 in, giving 12, 24 … 84 in, which is 7 plants. Rows of 8, 7 and 8 make <strong>23 positions</strong>.</li>
        <li>Rows across the width instead: 36 ÷ 12 = 3 steps gives 4 plants per row, and 84 ÷ 18 = 4.67 gives 4 full steps, so 5 rows. That is <strong>20 positions</strong>, with 12 in of length left over.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Press “Try an example” with imperial units selected to load these numbers. The spacings are only an illustration; use the figures for your own plants.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What this calculator does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It does not know any plant’s spacing.</strong> Prunest has no plant database. Spacing must come from your seed packet, plant label or supplier.</li>
      <li><strong>One plant type per layout.</strong> Straight and staggered rows are covered; beds mixing several plants at different spacings are not.</li>
      <li><strong>Rectangular beds only.</strong> For an L-shaped bed, lay out each rectangle separately.</li>
      <li><strong>Positions, not advice.</strong> The count is how many centres fit the geometry. Whether to fill every position is your call.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
